\description{
Pipe-friendly convenience wrapper around \code{\link[=augment_trends]{augment_trends()}} focused on a
single task: removing the trend from a time series. It adds a
\verb{detrend_\{method\}} column holding the detrended series (the deviation from
trend, often called the \emph{cycle} in economics) and, optionally, the
underlying trend itself.

For econometric filters such as \code{"hp"} (the default), \code{"bk"}, \code{"cf"}, and
\code{"hamilton"}, the detrended series is the business-cycle component those
filters were designed to isolate (e.g. the output gap).
}
\details{
\code{detrend_series()} is a thin wrapper: it calls \code{\link[=augment_trends]{augment_trends()}} with the
requested methods and subtracts each fitted trend from the series (on the
log scale when \code{transform = "log"}). All trend-fitting behaviour,
validation, grouping, and the unified parameters (\code{window}, \code{smoothing},
\code{band}, \code{align}, \code{params}) are inherited unchanged from \code{\link[=augment_trends]{augment_trends()}}.
See its documentation for method internals and parameter details.

Detrending does \strong{not} remove seasonality: the detrended series of a raw
seasonal series still contains the seasonal swings, and seasonality can
leak into the cycle estimated by filters such as HP. For seasonal data,
seasonally adjust first and detrend the adjusted series (see Examples), or
use \code{\link[=decompose_series]{decompose_series()}} for a full trend/seasonal/remainder split.
